course: dictionary
lesson: 1921
title: Comets: icy visitors from space
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A comet is a ball of ice, dust and rock that travels around the Sun. You can read the word in the sentence to help you work out what it means.
explain: When a comet comes close to the Sun, it heats up. It makes a glowing cloud and a long bright tail that we can sometimes see in the night sky.
explain: «Now and then one of these icy bodies falls in towards the Sun and becomes a comet.» The words icy and falls in tell you a comet starts out cold and comes closer to the Sun. A good check is to put your meaning in place of the word and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: A comet is made of ice, dust and rock.
fact: «Comet» is a noun, and its plural is comets.
fact: Halley's Comet comes back about every 76 years.
fact: Comets are different from meteors (shooting stars), which are tiny rocks burning up in our air, and from asteroids, which are rocky.
fact: Comets come from the cold, far outer parts of the solar system.
exercise: What is a comet made of? | Gold and glass | *Ice, dust and rock | Water from the sea | Fire and smoke
exercise: What happens to a comet when it comes close to the Sun? | It gets smaller and colder | It turns into a planet | *It heats up and grows a glowing cloud and a long tail | It freezes solid and disappears
exercise: «Meteor showers happen when the Earth passes through trails of dust left by comets.» Find and copy one word that means tiny bits of powder. | passes | *dust | Earth | showers
test: What is the difference between a comet and a meteor? | A comet and a meteor are the same thing | A meteor is a planet with a long tail | *A comet is ice, dust and rock that travels around the Sun, while a meteor is a tiny rock burning up in our air | A comet is a tiny rock burning up in our air, while a meteor is ice
test: How often does Halley's Comet come back? | Every year | *About every 76 years | Every 1000 years | Only once ever
exam: Using the clues from this lesson, what is a comet? | A tiny rock burning up in our air | A glowing cloud that stays still in the sky | A rocky planet with rings | *A ball of ice, dust and rock that travels around the Sun
